  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A front will continue to slowly move southeast\nof the area overnight. Showers will taper across Delmarva and \nNew Jersey late tonight.\n\nThe remaining part of the Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been\ncancelled. Low pressure passing north of northern New England \nwill continue to push a potent cold front offshore for the \nearly overnight period. The last of the thunderstorms have moved\noff the NJ/DE shore, but more scattered showers remain behind \nthe front will linger across southern NJ and Delmarva into the \novernight period. Likely POPS will remain S/E and chance pops\nfor the Delaware Valley areas. Additional rains will be rather\nlight, perhaps 0.1 to 0.2 inches. \n\nA tight pressure gradient then develops over the area overnight\nthrough Saturday as broad high pressure centered over the Southern\nPlains begins to build east. Gusty north to northwest winds will\ndevelop tonight and will continue through Saturday, with winds \nranging from 15 to 25 mph with 30 to 40 mph gusts. Much cooler \non Saturday with highs in the 50s and surface dew points falling\ntowards the 30s. Some showers may linger tonight into Saturday \nmorning especially across southern NJ and Delmarva as the front \nslows for a time, but they should clear out finally later in the\nday if not before.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freeze conditions are expected for \nSaturday night into Sunday morning with another round of frost \npossible for Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nThe coldest airmass of the season thus far will usher into the \nregion this weekend. With high pressure settling in from the north, \nskies will become clear by Saturday evening. While it will be a bit \nbreezy early in the night, the pressure gradient is expected to \nlessen overnight, especially for locales north and west of I-95. In \nthese areas, anticipate a period of good radiational cooling \nconditions where low temperatures are expected to fall into the \nupper 20s to low 30s. For this reason, have issued a Freeze Watch \nfor potions of eastern PA and northern NJ for Saturday night. \nFurther south and east, it appears that winds will remain elevated \nenough to preclude frost formation where temperatures will be in the \nmid to upper 30s. At this time, no frost/freeze headlines are \nexpected along the I-95 corridor and points south and east. \n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will then only be in the 50s for the \nentire region despite mostly sunny skies. Temperatures will then \nbegin to slowly moderate Sunday night into early next week. Another \nround of frost/freeze conditions will be possible for Sunday night \ninto Monday morning, mainly confined to the Lehigh Valley, Poconos \nand northern New Jersey. Lows are expected to be several degrees \nwarmer than Saturday night mainly in the mid to upper 30s, so this \nwould favor frost over freeze where the growing season remains \nactive. Too early to determine any headlines at this time, but will \nbe considered in future updates.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Uncertainty persists regarding a nor'easter \ndeveloping off the coast of the southern Mid-Atlantic that may cause \nunsettled conditions and hazardous marine impacts early next week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis will pass through the region later \nSaturday. As it does so, the trough axis will then cutoff into an \nupper level low which will meander over the southern Mid-Atlantic \ncoast into early next week. At the same time, strong and expansive \nhigh pressure will be building over the Ohio Valley. While \nconfidence is increasing regarding a nor'easter developing off the \nNorth Carolina coast later this weekend, it must be said there \nremains a vast amount of uncertainty with regards to potential \nimpacts to our region into next week. \n\nConsiderable differences amongst global/ensemble guidance exists \nwith regards to just how close/long this system will get/last in our \nregion. Some guidance keeps the system suppressed further south due \nto the strong high to the north, which will then minimize potential \nimpacts to our area. However, if the system is further north (and \nthis has been the trend over the past 24 hours), then more in the \nway of impacts and unsettled weather may occur. At the very least, \nit is expected that gusty northeast breezes will occur for the \nmajority of the week with the highest gusts located near coastal \nareas. While it isn't expected to be a washout by any means, there \nis at least a chance of showers each day for areas south and east of \nI-95 beginning as early as Sunday night through the end of the week. \nMore details to come over the weekend once forecast guidance comes \ninto better agreement.",
